---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/hgb/hgb333a.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  subtitle={metadata.nativeTitle}
  covers={metadata.covers}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope — Audit Committee Members Specifically" },
        { id: "s3", label: "History — the 2016 AReG, Not the 2021 FISG" },
        { id: "s4", label: "The Core Mechanism — Escalating an Administrative Violation Into a Crime" },
        { id: "s5", label: "§333a Nr.1 — Escalation Through Bribery" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Worked Example — an Audit Committee Member Bribed to Wave Through Prohibited Services" },
        { id: "s7", label: "§333a Nr.2 — Escalation Through Persistent Repetition" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Worked Example — Repeated Violations Without Any Bribe at All" },
        { id: "s9", label: "An Honest Flag — the Precise Content of §334(2a)" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Critical Judgments &amp; Practical Difficulties" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Relationship to IFRS" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Provisions — Cross-Reference Map" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Quick-Reference Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  

  <!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
  
  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="card ci">
      <div class="callout info">A CORRECTED ATTRIBUTION: while researching this poster, closer verification showed §333a actually traces to the 2016 AReG (Abschlussprüfungsreformgesetz) — a different, earlier reform than the 2021 FISG wave this series has repeatedly traced through §316a, §323, and §331a. Worth getting the history right rather than forcing a false continuity narrative.</div>

  
      <div class="ctitle ci">The audit committee's own criminal accountability, closing out this series' review of Book 3's Straftatbestände</div>
      <div class="principle">§§331, 331a, 332, and 333 have now covered company management, the Bilanzeid signer, and the auditor's own accuracy and confidentiality obligations. §333a addresses a genuinely different party: the audit COMMITTEE established under §324 (covered in the earlier Audit poster) — but only in a narrow, specific circumstance, where an otherwise merely administrative violation is escalated into genuine criminal liability.</div>
      <div class="elabel">HOW §333A MAPS TO GERMAN ACCOUNTING PRINCIPLES</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span><strong>Layered severity, matching culpability</strong> — an oversight lapse remains an administrative matter UNLESS it is bought (bribery) or becomes a genuine pattern (persistence), at which point the law escalates the response</span>
        <span><strong>Protecting the audit committee's own integrity</strong> — the SAME oversight function §324 established (monitoring auditor independence, non-audit services) is itself now protected from corruption of the overseers themselves</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cs">
      <div class="ctitle cs">What §333a covers, at a glance</div>
      <div class="principle">§333a criminalises a member of an audit committee established under §324(1) sentence 1 who EITHER (1) commits an act described in §334(2a) and receives or is promised a Vermögensvorteil (financial/property advantage) for it, OR (2) commits the SAME §334(2a) act persistently/repeatedly (beharrlich wiederholt) — either scenario punishable by imprisonment of up to ONE YEAR or a fine.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — Audit Committee Members Specifically</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A genuinely narrow Sonderdelikt, distinct from every other party this cluster of provisions covers</div>
      <div class="principle">§333a's Täterkreis is confirmed to be limited SPECIFICALLY to members of an audit committee (Prüfungsausschuss) established under §324(1) sentence 1 — established in the earlier Audit poster as the body (either a dedicated committee or the supervisory board itself performing this function) responsible for monitoring auditor independence, non-audit services, and the audit process generally. This is neither the auditor themselves (§332, §333), nor company management (§331), nor the specific Bilanzeid signer (§331a) — a genuinely distinct fifth category of potential offender.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>History — the 2016 AReG, Not the 2021 FISG</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A different reform than the one traced through the previous three posters</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§333a was introduced by the 2016 Abschlussprüfungsreformgesetz (AReG) — Germany's implementation of the EU's own 2014 Audit Reform Directive and Regulation — several years BEFORE the 2021 FISG reform this series has repeatedly traced through §316a's PIE definition, §323's tiered liability caps, and §331a's standalone Bilanzeid offence. Both reforms share a broadly similar underlying concern (strengthening audit quality and independence), but they are genuinely SEPARATE legislative interventions, several years apart — worth distinguishing clearly rather than assuming every audit-related criminal provision in this cluster traces to the same single reform.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The Core Mechanism — Escalating an Administrative Violation Into a Crime</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">§334(2a) itself is only an Ordnungswidrigkeit — §333a is what turns it criminal</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§334, covered in the NEXT poster in this series, generally defines administrative offences (Ordnungswidrigkeiten — punishable by a fine only, not imprisonment) for various breaches across this Subsection. §334(2a) specifically addresses conduct BY an audit committee member. On its own, this remains a purely administrative matter. §333a's genuinely distinctive contribution is to ESCALATE this same underlying conduct into full CRIMINAL liability, but ONLY in two specific, narrow circumstances — bribery (Nr.1) or persistent repetition (Nr.2) — reflecting a considered legislative judgement that an isolated, non-corrupt lapse should remain administrative, while a bought or genuinely repeated failure warrants the law's strongest response.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>§333a Nr.1 — Escalation Through Bribery</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A financial or property advantage received or promised in exchange for the violation</div>
      <div class="principle">§333a Nr.1 criminalises an audit committee member who commits a §334(2a) act AND receives, or is promised, a Vermögensvorteil (a financial or property advantage) for doing so — a genuinely corruption-flavoured trigger, structurally similar to the "gegen Entgelt" aggravation this series has now seen repeatedly across §332(2) and §333(2), but here operating as the trigger for CRIMINALITY itself, rather than merely an aggravating factor on top of an already-criminal base offence.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Worked Example — an Audit Committee Member Bribed to Wave Through Prohibited Services</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">A payment specifically for looking the other way on independence rules</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 1: Approving a prohibited non-audit service in exchange for a personal benefit</div>
        <div class="wk-body">An audit committee member, responsible for overseeing the auditor's independence per §324, improperly approves a non-audit service that §319's independence rules (established in the earlier Audit poster) would ordinarily prohibit — and does so after quietly receiving a personal financial benefit from the auditing firm in exchange.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">Assuming this conduct itself qualifies as a §334(2a) violation, the RECEIPT of the personal financial benefit transforms what would otherwise be a purely administrative (Bußgeld) matter into a genuine CRIMINAL offence under §333a Nr.1 — punishable by imprisonment of up to one year or a fine, REGARDLESS of how modest the benefit received actually was.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>§333a Nr.2 — Escalation Through Persistent Repetition</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">No bribe required — sheer, sustained repetition is itself enough</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§333a Nr.2 supplies a genuinely SEPARATE trigger, requiring NO bribery or corrupt motive at all — an audit committee member who commits the SAME §334(2a) act "beharrlich" (persistently/stubbornly) REPEATEDLY escalates their own conduct into criminal territory purely through the SUSTAINED pattern itself, reflecting a legislative judgement that repeated, wilful disregard of the same oversight duty is qualitatively different from — and more serious than — a single isolated administrative lapse, even absent any financial corruption.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — Repeated Violations Without Any Bribe at All</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Genuine, sustained pattern alone crosses the criminal threshold</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 2: A committee member who repeatedly ignores the same oversight duty</div>
        <div class="wk-body">An audit committee member, without any bribe or corrupt motive whatsoever, simply and repeatedly fails to properly review non-audit service pre-approvals over several consecutive reporting periods — not through a single oversight, but through a genuinely sustained pattern of disregarding the same specific duty each time.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">DESPITE the complete absence of any financial benefit or corrupt intent, the PERSISTENT, repeated nature of the same underlying §334(2a) violation itself satisfies §333a Nr.2 — escalating what began as a series of individually administrative lapses into a genuine criminal offence, purely through the sustained pattern.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>An Honest Flag — the Precise Content of §334(2a)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">The mechanism is confirmed; the exact underlying conduct is worth dedicated verification</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">This poster's research confirmed §333a's own STRUCTURE (Täterkreis, the two escalation triggers, the one-year maximum penalty) with strong confidence, drawn directly from the current statutory text. What it did NOT fully confirm is the PRECISE, complete definition of the underlying §334(2a) conduct itself — the specific audit-committee-related act that, absent bribery or persistence, would remain a mere administrative matter. Given §334 is the subject of the NEXT poster in this series, this specific point is best resolved there, with dedicated attention, rather than asserted with false confidence here.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Practical Difficulties</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>What genuinely counts as "beharrlich" (persistent) repetition, as distinct from a series of isolated, unconnected lapses</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">PRACTICAL DIFFICULTIES</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Proving the causal link between a specific benefit received and the specific §334(2a) act, as distinct from a benefit received for entirely unrelated reasons</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Audit committee corruption sits entirely outside any accounting standard</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Precisely as with every other criminal provision this series has documented, IFRS itself has no equivalent — corporate governance oversight bodies and their own criminal accountability are governed entirely by each jurisdiction's own company and criminal law. §333a is a genuinely narrow but distinctive illustration of how far German law extends the audit-quality accountability structure this series has documented — reaching not just the preparer and the auditor, but the AUDIT COMMITTEE that oversees the auditor's own independence.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Where every §333a event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event / obligation</span><span>Document affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Bribed audit committee approval</span><span>—</span><span>No specific financial statement directly affected; the oversight process itself is corrupted</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Persistent unremedied violation</span><span>—</span><span>Escalates purely through sustained pattern, independent of any specific document</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Provisions — Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">How §333a connects across HGB Book 3</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Provision</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§324</span><span>Establishes the audit committee whose members §333a specifically targets</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§319</span><span>The independence rules an audit committee's oversight function most directly protects</span></div>
        <div class="tr"><span>§334(2a)</span><span>Supplies the underlying administrative conduct §333a escalates into a crime — covered fully in the next poster</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§332, §333</span><span>The auditor's own parallel criminal offences, targeting a genuinely different party within the same broader audit ecosystem</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="three-col">
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Prüfungsausschuss</div><div class="glossary-def">Audit committee, established under §324(1) sentence 1 — §333a's specific Täterkreis.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Vermögensvorteil</div><div class="glossary-def">A financial or property advantage — the corrupt benefit that triggers §333a Nr.1's escalation.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Beharrlich wiederholen</div><div class="glossary-def">To persistently/stubbornly repeat — the sustained-pattern trigger under §333a Nr.2, requiring no corrupt motive.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Ordnungswidrigkeit</div><div class="glossary-def">An administrative offence, punishable by fine only — what §334(2a) conduct remains absent the §333a escalation triggers.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">AReG</div><div class="glossary-def">Abschlussprüfungsreformgesetz — the 2016 reform implementing EU Audit Reform requirements, which introduced §333a.</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
